n Texas Hold'em, typically ranked 3rd-5th best. It has excellent potential to make top pair, straights, and has high card value.\n\n**Position Analysis**: My opponent checked (action: 0), meaning they limped from the small blind position. This is often a sign of weakness or a trap with a premium hand, but most commonly indicates a mediocre holding.\n\n**Promising Moves**:\n\n1. **Check (0)**: See the flop for free, maintain pot control. However, this gives my opponent a free chance to improve with any two cards, which wastes the strength of AK.\n\n2. **Raise to 200-400**: A standard 2-4x big blind raise would build the pot with a premium hand and potentially take it down pre-flop or get value from weaker hands. This is strategically sound.\n\n3. **All-in (-2)**: Extremely aggressive with 19,900 chips. This would only get called by premium pairs (AA, KK, QQ) where I'm behind, or AK where we chop. It's an overplay that folds out hands I dominate.\n\n**Opponent's Check Analysis**: The limp suggests they likely have a speculative hand (suited connectors, small pairs, weak aces) or are slow-playing a monster. A standard raise will define their hand strength.\n\nReason: \n\nAK is a premium hand that plays best by building the pot and taking initiative. A raise to 300-400 chips (3-4x BB assuming 100 BB) accomplishes multiple goals: it builds value when ahead, defines opponent's range, and takes control of the hand. Checking wastes positional advantage and hand strength. All-in is unnecessarily risky and only gets called by hands that beat or tie us.\n\nAnswer: 300"}
